<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nukus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nukus is the capital of the Republic of Karakalpakstan in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>. A small settlement until the Soviet period, it replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turtkul">Turtkul</a> as capital in 1939 and grew into an industrial city. It is known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savitsky-museum">Savitsky Museum</a> of Soviet avant-garde art, and in 2022 it saw deadly protests against plans to end Karakalpakstan's right to secede.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nukus.jpg" alt="Nukus" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aziko9 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berdaq_at%C4%B1nda%C7%B5%C4%B1_Qaraqalpaq_%C3%A1debiyat%C4%B1_tariyx%C4%B1_m%C3%A1mleketlik_muzeyi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nukus is the capital and largest city of the autonomous Republic of Karakalpakstan and the sixth-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> passes west of it. The name comes from the tribal name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakalpaks">Karakalpaks</a>, literally New Kath, in Persian Nūkās; the root kus derives from Kath, the name of the past capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, now called Beruniy. The city has a cold desert climate, with long, dry and very hot summers and short, cold and snowy winters. Because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a> and the Amu Darya have dried up, the climate has become much hotter and drier since 1960, and health conditions resulting from salt and other chemicals in the air have become more common.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In its modern location Nukus began as a fortress built in the 17th century. It grew slowly within the Khivan Khanate and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and was a mid-sized city before the Soviet era. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakalpak-autonomous-oblast">Karakalpak Autonomous Oblast</a> was established, Toʻrtkoʻl became its administrative centre. In the 1920s, however, Toʻrtkoʻl was threatened by flooding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>, whose banks lay about 12 kilometres away, and most of the institutions of Karakalpakstan moved towards Nukus. The city was officially founded in 1932 and eventually received capital status. By the 1950s further development and industrialisation had made it a city of broad avenues and big public buildings.</p><p>The city's isolation made it the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>'s Chemical Research Institute, a major research and testing centre for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemical-weapons">chemical weapons</a>. In 2002 the United States Department of Defense dismantled the institute, which had been a major research and testing site for the Novichok agent, under a Cooperative Threat Reduction programme costing six million dollars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nukus is the centre of Karakalpakstan's economy, government, politics and culture. It is best known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savitsky-museum">Nukus Museum of Art</a>, named after Igor Savitsky, which is noted for its collection of modern Russian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> art from 1918 to 1935. Both Savitsky and the collection survived because the city's remoteness limited the reach of the Soviet authorities. The State Museum holds archaeological finds, traditional jewellery, costumes and musical instruments, and displays on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a>. The Berdakh museum, built in 1998, honours the Karakalpak poet Berdakh. Near the city lie the Zoroastrian structure of Shylpyk, raised in the 2nd to 4th centuries as a dakhma, and the settlement of Tok-kala.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nukus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its capital moved from Turtkul to <b>Nukus</b> in 1939.</blockquote><a href="/state/karakalpakstan">Karakalpakstan</a></li></ul></section>
